# Every variable the app reads. Keep this file in sync — it is the checklist for a new environment.
# Real values go in .env.local, which is gitignored and never committed.
# On Railway these go in the service's Variables tab, not in a file. See docs/DEPLOY.md.

# --- Database (Neon) -------------------------------------------------------
# Neon dashboard -> your project -> Connection string.
# Use the DIRECT (unpooled) string: this app is a persistent Node process with
# its own pool, so PgBouncer would add a hop and buy nothing (DECISIONS D13).
# Keep sslmode=verify-full rather than require — pg 9 will weaken what `require`
# means, and writing the stronger mode now keeps today's behaviour (D58).
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host.neon.tech/dbname?sslmode=verify-full"
# Neon's POOLED string. Read by nothing; kept because a future serverless path
# would need it and finding it again costs a trip to the dashboard (D13).
DATABASE_URL_POOLED=""

# --- Auth (Better Auth) ----------------------------------------------------
# Generated with: openssl rand -base64 32
# A different value in production than in development, and changing it signs
# everyone out.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=""
# The app's own origin, with no trailing slash. http://localhost:3000 in dev,
# https://<your-app>.up.railway.app (or your own domain) in production. Better
# Auth builds its OAuth callback URLs from this, so if it disagrees with what is
# registered in the GitHub and Google consoles, sign-in fails and nothing else does.
BETTER_AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# github.com/settings/developers -> New OAuth App
# Authorization callback URL must be exactly: {BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/github
# One OAuth App per origin: localhost and production cannot share one, because
# GitHub allows a single callback URL per app.
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=""
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# console.cloud.google.com -> Credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Web application
# Authorized redirect URI must be exactly: {BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/google
# Google accepts several redirect URIs on one client, so dev and production may
# share it if you prefer.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# --- LLM (two named providers, plus one escape hatch) ----------------------
# Needed from Step 4. Paste a key for whichever you have. The app offers only
# the providers it has a key for, and when it has none it says so in words
# rather than showing a choice it cannot honour.
#
# Only the KEY is required. The base URL and the model name have defaults in
# src/lib/llm/config.ts, taken from each vendor's own documentation: an address
# nobody can look up is not a secret, it is a thing to get wrong. Both stay
# overridable below, so moving off a model is a config change, not a deploy.
#
# Pin ONE serving endpoint. Do not use a router: MiMo-V2.5's capabilities differ
# by provider, and a router can land on a tool-less provider between runs, which
# would make the Q&A agent work one day and silently fail the next (D44).

# Which provider answers when nobody has chosen one: mimo | gemini | custom.
# Blank means the first one that has a key, in that order. Naming one you have
# no key for falls through to one you do, rather than leaving the app with no
# model at all over a typo.
LLM_DEFAULT=""

# MiMo, on Xiaomi's own endpoint. Defaults to https://api.xiaomimimo.com/v1 and
# the model mimo-v2.5 — the cheap one that does tool calling, not the roughly
# tenfold code/agent model mimo-v2.5-pro (D45).
LLM_MIMO_API_KEY=""
LLM_MIMO_BASE_URL=""
LLM_MIMO_MODEL=""
# Whether this endpoint advertises json_schema structured outputs. Leave blank
# to take the verified default, which is false here: Xiaomi's API reference
# documents tools and a text response format, and nothing finer (D47).
LLM_MIMO_SUPPORTS_JSON_SCHEMA=""

# Gemini, through Google's OpenAI-compatibility layer. Defaults to
# https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai and the model
# gemini-3.8-flash.
LLM_GEMINI_API_KEY=""
LLM_GEMINI_BASE_URL=""
LLM_GEMINI_MODEL=""
# Defaults to true here, unlike MiMo: Google documents response_format with a
# schema on this endpoint. That difference is exactly why the flag belongs to
# the provider instead of being one global setting — one value would have been
# right for one of them and silently wrong for the other.
LLM_GEMINI_SUPPORTS_JSON_SCHEMA=""

# Any other OpenAI-compatible endpoint. No defaults, so these three go together:
# a key with nowhere to send it is treated as no provider at all, because a
# half-configured model would fail at the moment someone asks it something
# rather than at the moment it was set up.
LLM_BASE_URL=""
LLM_API_KEY=""
LLM_MODEL=""
# False unless you say otherwise, and the app sends this endpoint no thinking
# setting whatsoever: we know nothing about it, and an unrecognised parameter
# is a 400 on a strict server.
LLM_SUPPORTS_JSON_SCHEMA=""

# --- Sandbox (Step 6 stretch only) -----------------------------------------
# e2b.dev -> Dashboard -> API keys. Validated by src/lib/env.ts as optional and
# read by no feature yet.
E2B_API_KEY=""

# --- Set by the platform, not by you ---------------------------------------
# Listed so that nobody sets them by hand and wonders why the app stopped
# answering. The Dockerfile and Railway between them already supply these.
#
# PORT       Railway injects it and the standalone server listens on it. Do not
#            pin it in the Variables tab — if the platform's value and yours
#            disagree, the health check goes to a port nothing is listening on.
# HOSTNAME   Must be 0.0.0.0 so the server accepts connections from outside the
#            container. The Dockerfile sets it.
# NODE_ENV   "production" in the image. Also what makes src/db/index.ts stop
#            caching the pool on globalThis, which is a dev-only reload guard.

# --- Multi-instance only ---------------------------------------------------
# Next generates a Server Action encryption key per build. With more than one
# replica, or during a rolling deploy where an open tab meets a new build, the
# two sides disagree and Server Actions fail with "Failed to find Server Action".
# Fixing it means pinning the key AT BUILD TIME (the Dockerfile takes it as a
# build argument; setting it only at runtime does nothing, because it is
# compiled into the build output).
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
NEXT_SERVER_ACTIONS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# --- Tests only ------------------------------------------------------------
# Opt-in gates, both read by *.test.ts and by nothing that ships. Never set
# either of these on the server: VESTRA_DB points tests at a real DATABASE_URL
# and they write to it.
# VESTRA_DB=1     run the tests that need a live Postgres
# VESTRA_LIVE=1   run the tests that reach the real GitHub and LLM endpoints
